 See the focused examples for [models](../examples/sdk/02-custom-model.ts), [tools](../examples/sdk/05-tools.ts), [extensions](../examples/sdk/06-extensions.ts), and [full control](../examples/sdk/12-full-control.ts).
 
+## Final local Bash admission
+
+SDK-created standard-local Bash definitions support dispatcher-requested admission,
+including the **original configured default** inside `createAgentSession()`. Return
+an admission requirement from the hook; do not replace that owner or reconstruct its
+captured shell/prefix/options. Capability follows its actual execute function and
+`createLocalBashOperations()` backend, not its name or metadata. Custom/opaque
+backends remain unsupported and are rejected before invocation when admission is requested.
+
+`requireAdmission: true` is a separate, stricter direct-call rule. It is not needed
+to make the canonical default eligible:
+
+| Call | Default factory | `requireAdmission: true` |
+|---|---|---|
+| Ordinary direct/legacy call without admission context | Allowed, before and after guarded use of the same instance | Rejected |
+| Dispatched call requiring admission | Final policy/gate required | Final policy/gate required |
+| Guarded forwarding call losing or substituting context | Rejected before backend effects | Rejected before backend effects |
+
+There is no sticky activation or factory mode change. Existing options/backend
+instances, legacy four-argument AgentTool calls and receivers remain intact.
+
+A host `beforeToolCall` hook, or extension `tool_call` handler, returns
+`{ admission: { isValid, evaluate, approve } }` for **every invocation** on the guarded
+path, not only raw commands already classified unsafe. This is a requirement, not
+an allow decision. `isValid(): boolean` must compare captured policy/session
+generations with current ones. Optional `evaluate(plan, presentationContext)` runs
+final policy and returns `"allow"`, `"deny"`, or `"confirm"` (synchronously or asynchronously).
+`"allow"` permits a safe final plan without UI; `"deny"` starts no process and never
+asks for confirmation. Omission means `"confirm"`.
+`approve(plan: unknown, presentationContext?: unknown): Promise<boolean>` must return
+exactly `true` for a fresh affirmative decision when final policy requires confirmation. The native local Bash gate supplies
+an immutable `LocalBashExecutionPlan` and this invocation's `ExtensionToolContext`.
+The plan includes final command, cwd, effective timeout in milliseconds, shell,
+argv and command transport, after prefix/spawnHook/backend preparation. It omits
+environment values; do not display secrets in confirmation UI.
+
+Confirmation requires dialog-capable UI. Pending, denied, missing required UI, thrown decisions,
+cancellation, stale policy/session/registry, replaced executor/backend, and changed
+prepared values start no process. One affirmative decision admits one captured
+invocation. Concurrent calls and reused textual IDs do not share decisions.
+
+### Presentation wrappers
+
+`bindToolExecutionContext(wrapperExecute, delegateExecute, isValid): void` binds a
+known forwarding function to its exact native delegate. Both arguments are function
+objects, never tool names, ToolInfo, schema markers, or model arguments. A wrapper
+must forward the fifth AgentTool argument unchanged; definition wrappers forward
+`ctx.toolExecutionContext` on the extension context. Bind each forwarding layer,
+and make `isValid` check that its actual delegate is still the captured function.
+SDK definition/registered wrappers bind themselves and preserve lazy UI/session
+context getters. Unknown or replaced functions are rejected before invocation.
+
+The dispatcher enters an actual-function-bound native invocation scope **before**
+forwarders run. Standard-local Bash compares that lifecycle scope with the explicitly
+forwarded context. Missing or mismatched context rejects before the backend; Bash
+does not retrieve a lost frame from ambient storage to approve the call. The final
+prepared-plan gate still uses only the explicitly forwarded frame.
+
+Every dispatched legacy/non-admission call enters an explicit undefined native
+scope, including nested unbound executors. Native async-local isolation restores
+the caller's scope on return/error/cancellation and keeps concurrent reused IDs
+independent. Outside guarded execution, direct calls on the same default instance
+remain legacy calls; no global approval ledger or transferable consent is created.
+Agent-core invokes native scope hooks without depending on Node; only local Bash
+uses Node async-local storage. Native backend registration may supply that optional
+scope runner; it is privileged lifecycle plumbing, not a GP capability shortcut.
+
+Binding does not make arbitrary forwarding code safe. Native extension code is
+privileged; it can spawn processes itself. This contract is **not a sandbox** against
+malicious native extensions, nor does it approve `user_bash`, `pi.exec`, or arbitrary
+custom operations. Hard-block hooks retain precedence over admission requests.
+
